>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `linarithLib`

``` hol4
structure linarithLib
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Provides registry-driven, kernel-checked linear arithmetic automation.

The search engine treats equalities, disequalities, strict inequalities,
and non-strict inequalities as linear constraints. Its untrusted
Fourier--Motzkin search produces certificates which are replayed by HOL's
kernel. Literal coefficients are supported; a non-linear subterm is
otherwise treated as an atomic expression rather than normalised as a
non-linear polynomial.

Loading `linarithLib` installs the natural-number instance. The optional
structures `intLinarith`, `realLinarith`, and `ratLinarith` install the
integer, real, and rational instances respectively when loaded. They are
built in `src/auto/linarith/instances`; add that directory to the HOL load
path before loading them. Mixed-carrier problems also require the
instances at both ends of the relevant registered injection.

The public surface consists of the full and simple tactics, a
configuration-explicit tactic, a forward prover, a conversion, a simpset
fragment, and an unsafe simplifier solver. `LINARITH_ss` is not part of
`srw_ss()`. The solver, whose name is `"lin_arith"`, is already installed
in the clasimp and Aesop simpsets.

Persistent `[arith]` declarations supply extra facts. Persistent
`[arith_split]` declarations supply P-form operator-splitting rules to the
full tactics. A `markerLib.Split th` argument supplies such a rule for one
full-tactic invocation. Instance `pre_split` rules handle operators such
as `MIN`, `MAX`, natural subtraction, and absolute value. The `[arith]`
and `[arith_split]` sets both initially have no members.

Instances also supply facts about individual atoms — division, modulus,
and `Num` facts for the shipped ones. The full tactics add them for the
atoms occurring in the goal, repeating until no new fact appears. They do
so only where the arithmetic has failed and no case split remains: the
full tactics split on demand, refuting what they have before expanding
any disjunction or operator.

The trace named `"linarith"` reports preprocessing, search, and replay
detail at levels 1 through 3.

### Failure

Individual operations fail when no registered instance covers a relation,
when preprocessing or certificate search finds no proof, or when a
supplied marker or split theorem is malformed.

### Example

``` hol4
Theorem linarith_overview_example:
  !x y z : num. x <= y /\ y < z ==> x < z
Proof
  rpt strip_tac >>
  linarithLib.LINARITH_TAC []
QED
```

